text:
Decorate a FastAPI view to render its return value through a Chameleon template.


Usage
Source


template(template_file: Optional[Union[Callable[..., R], str]] = None, mimetype: str = 'text/html') -&gt; Callable[[Callable[P, R]], Callable[P, R]]
template(f: Callable[P, R]) -&gt; Callable[P, R]


Works on both sync and async view functions, and can be used three ways:
@template('home/index.pt')   # explicit template file
@template()                  # infer the template name
@template                    # bare form, also infers the name
When no template file is given, the name is inferred as {module}/{function} under the template folder (the module’s last dotted segment and the view’s name), preferring a .html file and falling back to .pt.
The decorated view should return a dict (used as the template’s variables). If it returns a fastapi.Response instead, the template is skipped and the response is passed through unchanged. Returning anything else raises FastAPIChameleonException.
Example:
@app.get('/')
@fastapi_chameleon.template('home/index.pt')
def index():
    return {'title': 'Home'}


Parameters


template_file: Optional[Union[Callable[…, R], str]] = None

The Chameleon template file (path relative to the template folder). Omit it (or pass None) to infer the name from the view. In the bare @template form this argument receives the view function itself.

mimetype: str = "text/html"

The response media type. Defaults to text/html.





Returns




The decorated view (bare form) or a decorator to apply to the view.

Quick start
A minimal but complete app in two files.
main.py
from pathlib import Path

import fastapi
import uvicorn

import fastapi_chameleon

app = fastapi.FastAPI()

# Point the engine at your template folder (do this before views are registered).
BASE_DIR = Path(__file__).resolve().parent
fastapi_chameleon.global_init(BASE_DIR / 'templates', auto_reload=True)


@app.get('/')
@fastapi_chameleon.template('index.pt')
def hello_world():
    return {'message': "Let's go Chameleon and FastAPI!"}


if __name__ == '__main__':
    uvicorn.run(app, host='127.0.0.1', port=8000)
templates/index.pt
&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;
&lt;html lang="en"&gt;
&lt;head&gt;
    &lt;meta charset="UTF-8"&gt;
    &lt;title&gt;Title&lt;/title&gt;
&lt;/head&gt;
&lt;body&gt;
&lt;h1&gt;Hello world&lt;/h1&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Your message is &lt;strong&gt;${message}&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/body&gt;
&lt;/html&gt;
Run it with python main.py (or uvicorn main:app) and visit http://127.0.0.1:8000. The dict returned from the view becomes the template’s variables: {'message': ...} renders into ${message}.
Chameleon templates are plain HTML5 with ${expr} interpolation plus the full TAL attribute language (tal:repeat, tal:content, and friends) in either .pt or .html files. The Writing Templates guide covers the syntax and shared layouts.
Note the decorator order: the route decorator (@app.get(...)) goes on the outside, and @fastapi_chameleon.template(...) is applied directly to the view function.

Manual rendering with response()
If you need full manual control without going through the decorator, say for a non-200 status code or a non-HTML mimetype, use response():
import fastapi_chameleon

@router.get('/report')
def report():
    return fastapi_chameleon.response('reports/summary.pt',
                                      status_code=202,
                                      title='Monthly summary')
It renders the template with the keyword arguments as the model and wraps the result in a fastapi.Response with your chosen mimetype (default 'text/html') and status_code (default 200).

Every FastAPI app is born speaking JSON. Ask it for /, and it answers with curly braces. That is perfect for an API and slightly absurd for a website, where the thing on the other end of the request is a person with a browser, expecting a page.
fastapi-chameleon teaches your app a second language. You keep writing FastAPI views the way you always have, with path parameters, dependencies, async if you like. The difference is one decorator on top, and instead of returning JSON you return a plain dict. The decorator hands that dict to a Chameleon template, the template turns it into HTML, and FastAPI sends the page. Views decide, templates show. That is the whole arrangement.
Throughout this guide we will build a small site for a secondhand bookshop called Shelf Life. It has a home page, a catalog, a login form, and the occasional book that sold yesterday and now needs a graceful 404. Every mechanism in the package shows up somewhere in that story.
If Jinja is your template language of choice, the sister project fastapi-jinja has the same shape and the same decorator-first design. Everything here will feel familiar there.

Install
uv add fastapi-chameleon
# or
pip install fastapi-chameleon
Python 3.10 or newer. The package depends on exactly two things, fastapi and chameleon, and nothing else. You also need an ASGI server such as uvicorn to actually serve requests.


A first page
Two files are enough to open the shop. The template folder is handed to global_init() once, and from then on every template path you write is relative to it.
main.py
from pathlib import Path

import fastapi
import uvicorn

import fastapi_chameleon

app = fastapi.FastAPI()

# Point the engine at your template folder before any view is decorated.
BASE_DIR = Path(__file__).resolve().parent
fastapi_chameleon.global_init(BASE_DIR / 'templates', auto_reload=True)


@app.get('/')
@fastapi_chameleon.template('index.pt')
def hello_world():
    return {'message': "Let's go Chameleon and FastAPI!"}


if __name__ == '__main__':
    uvicorn.run(app, host='127.0.0.1', port=8000)
templates/index.pt
&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;
&lt;html lang="en"&gt;
&lt;head&gt;
    &lt;meta charset="UTF-8"&gt;
    &lt;title&gt;Shelf Life&lt;/title&gt;
&lt;/head&gt;
&lt;body&gt;
&lt;h1&gt;Hello world&lt;/h1&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Your message is &lt;strong&gt;${message}&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/body&gt;
&lt;/html&gt;
Run it with python main.py or uvicorn main:app and open http://127.0.0.1:8000. Both commands work here for a reason worth noticing: global_init() runs at import time, before the decorated view is defined. Keep that order and the package never surprises you.


What you get back
The decorated function no longer returns a dict. It returns a fastapi.Response carrying a 200 status, a text/html content type, and this body:
&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;
&lt;html lang="en"&gt;
&lt;head&gt;
    &lt;meta charset="UTF-8"&gt;
    &lt;title&gt;Shelf Life&lt;/title&gt;
&lt;/head&gt;
&lt;body&gt;
&lt;h1&gt;Hello world&lt;/h1&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Your message is &lt;strong&gt;Let's go Chameleon and FastAPI!&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/body&gt;
&lt;/html&gt;
Each key in the dict became a variable in the template. ${message} was replaced by the string the view returned. There is no context object to build, no TemplateResponse to construct, no request to thread through by hand. The dict is the model.
Two details in that example are small now and important later:

Decorator order. The route decorator @app.get(...) is on the outside. @fastapi_chameleon.template(...) sits directly on the function. FastAPI must see the already-decorated view.
auto_reload=True. Chameleon compiles templates once and caches them, which is what you want in production. While you are editing templates every thirty seconds, you want them re-read. This flag is the switch. See Configuration.



